---
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import {
  arch02HappyPath,
  pickAndPlaceInspect,
  pickAndPlaceSimulate,
  pickAndPlaceValidate,
  versionOutput,
} from '../data/examples';
import { CORE_REPO, specs, versions } from '../data/facts';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Get Started"
  description="Install the RoboLang developer tools from a clone of the repository, validate and inspect your first task file and run it in a symbolic simulation."
>
  <PageHead slot="head" index="02" label="Get Started" title="Get started">
    Install the developer tools, check your first task file and run it in a symbolic simulation. This takes a few minutes and needs no robot, no external simulator and no ROS 2.
  </PageHead>

  <div class="prose">

    <h2>Requirements</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Python {versions.pythonMinimum} or newer</li>
      <li>Git</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The package has no runtime dependencies. It is tested on Ubuntu and Windows with Python 3.11
      and 3.13.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Install</h2>
    <p>
      RoboLang is installed from a clone of the repository. It is <strong>not published on PyPI</strong
      >, so <code>pip install robolang</code> will not install this project.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`git clone ${CORE_REPO}.git`} />
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="cd RoboLang" />
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="python -m pip install ." />
    <p>
      Installing into a <a href="https://docs.python.org/3/library/venv.html">virtual environment</a>
      is recommended. Then confirm the command is available:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="robolang --version" />
    <CodeBlock code={versionOutput} label="Output" />

    <h2>2. Validate a task file</h2>
    <p>The repository includes a small example. From the clone, run:</p>
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="robolang validate examples/pick_and_place.rob" />
    <CodeBlock code={pickAndPlaceValidate} label="Output" />
    <p>Three things to notice in that output:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The file is <strong>valid</strong>. <code>validate</code> checked its syntax, names, types and
        values.
      </li>
      <li>
        There is one <strong>warning</strong>, <code>W401</code>. The example writes
        <code>max_speed 0.5</code> with no unit, which the language still accepts but has
        deprecated. Writing <code>0.5 m/s</code> removes the warning. Warnings do not make a file
        invalid.
      </li>
      <li>
        The last line is printed every time: validation covers the text of the program only. It does
        not make a task safe to run on a robot.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. Inspect what it describes</h2>
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="robolang inspect examples/pick_and_place.rob" />
    <CodeBlock code={pickAndPlaceInspect} label="Output" />
    <p>
      <code>inspect</code> validates first, then prints the tasks in the file. Nothing is run. To
      get the same information as JSON for another program, add <code>--json</code>:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="robolang inspect examples/pick_and_place.rob --json" />
    <p>
      The JSON is tagged with its schema version. The <a href="/examples/#json-ir"
        >Examples page shows the result</a
      >, and the <a href={specs.irSchema}>IR schema specification</a> defines it. Use
      <code>--task NAME</code> to show a single task from a file that contains several.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Simulate it</h2>
    <p>
      <code>simulate</code> runs a validated task against a world file that declares the robots,
      objects, regions and sensors. The repository includes one:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="bash"
      code="robolang simulate examples/pick_and_place.rob --world tests/fixtures/simulation/worlds/base.json"
    />
    <CodeBlock code={pickAndPlaceSimulate} label="Output (standard error)" />
    <p>
      The summary line goes to standard error and the full run record, as JSON, to standard output.
      The run record lists every state, precondition and step and the final world. The same source,
      world, task and <code>--seed</code> always give the same record and run ID. The simulation is
      symbolic: no motion planning, kinematics, collisions or physics, and a simulated success is not
      evidence that a task is safe on hardware. The <a href="/examples/#simulate">Examples page</a>
      shows the record and a failing run.
    </p>

    <h2 id="arch02">5. Run the reference simulation (experimental)</h2>
    <p>
      <code>arch02</code> runs one case of the RFC-001 reference scenario. An AI planner stub asks a
      fixed arm to deliver a container, the arm hands custody to a mobile robot, and every message is a
      checked Core {versions.core} document.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="bash"
      code="robolang arch02 --scenario tests/fixtures/arch02/scenario.json --cases tests/fixtures/arch02/cases.json --case happy_path"
    />
    <CodeBlock code={arch02HappyPath} label="Output (standard error)" />
    <p>
      This is simulation only: one process, a symbolic world, no network and simulation-only
      signatures. The same scenario, case and seed give a byte-identical run record. Other cases in
      the same file, such as <code>v06_partition_before_release</code>, inject faults.
    </p>

    <h2>Exit status</h2>
    <p>
      <code>validate</code> and <code>inspect</code> return the same exit status, so they can be used
      in scripts and CI.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Status</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>The file is valid. Warnings do not change this.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>The file is not valid. Diagnostics are printed to stderr.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>2</code></td><td>Bad arguments, an unreadable file or an unknown task name.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p><code>simulate</code> reports how the run ended:</p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Status</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>The run succeeded.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>The source file is not valid.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>2</code></td><td>Usage error.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>3</code></td><td>The world file is not valid.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>4</code></td><td>The run failed.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>5</code></td><td>The run was cancelled.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      <code>arch02</code> returns <code>0</code> when the case ran, whatever outcome it recorded,
      <code>2</code> for a usage error and <code>3</code> for an invalid scenario or cases file.
    </p>

    <h2 id="versions">Version numbers</h2>
    <p>
      <code>robolang --version</code> prints the distribution, language and IR schema versions. They
      identify different things and change independently.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Version</th>
            <th scope="col">Current</th>
            <th scope="col">What it identifies</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Distribution</th>
            <td><code>{versions.distribution}</code></td>
            <td>The installable Python package. <code>a1</code> means first alpha, a pre-release.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Language</th>
            <td><code>{versions.language}</code></td>
            <td>
              The syntax and rules of <code>.rob</code> files. A file is 1.2 only if it starts with the
              marker <code>robolang 1.2;</code>.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">IR schema</th>
            <td><code>{versions.irSchema}</code></td>
            <td>
              The shape of the JSON printed by <code>inspect --json</code>: 1.0 for a 1.1 source, 1.1
              for a 1.2 source.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">World file and run record</th>
            <td><code>{versions.worldFile}</code> / <code>{versions.runRecord}</code></td>
            <td>
              The input and output of <code>simulate</code>. The run record is 1.0 for a 1.1 source and
              1.1 for a 1.2 source.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Core</th>
            <td><code>{versions.core}</code></td>
            <td>The experimental message and capability contracts used by <code>arch02</code>.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      So "RoboLang 1.2" refers to the language, while the software you installed is the alpha
      distribution {versions.distribution}.
    </p>

    <h2>Without installing</h2>
    <p>The same commands run straight from the clone:</p>
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="python src/robolang validate examples/pick_and_place.rob" />

    <div class="note">
      <p>
        <strong>What is installed.</strong> The installed package is the <code>robolang</code> tooling
        only: lexer, parser, validator, IR, the symbolic simulator, the Core {versions.core} checks and
        the reference simulation, behind the four commands. The repository also contains an older
        v1 prototype interpreter and an unvalidated ROS 2 adapter. They are kept as historical source,
        are not installed and are not supported. The
        <a href={specs.readme}>core README</a> explains the split.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Next</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/examples/">Examples</a>: valid and invalid task files and simulations, with real tool output.</li>
      <li><a href="/reference/">Language reference</a>: where the grammar and rules are specified.</li>
      <li><a href="/contribute/">Contribute</a>: report a problem or suggest a change.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Base>
